From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: the before-play pass stops waiting for builds that do not return (EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_PREPASS_JOB_WAIT_S, 120 s): the keys get their strike and the game starts

Switch: EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_PREPASS_JOB_WAIT_S=<seconds> (default 120; 0 = wait without a limit, as
0770 did). Only in the before-play pass (EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_PREPASS=1); the pass beside the game
waits as before. Prepass off: nothing changes.

Why (open fault of the T157 review): "a driver build that never returns hangs the boot". The
pass waited without a limit for the jobs in flight at the end of each pass and for a free slot,
and neither EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET_PREPASS_MAX_S nor the skip file could end those waits. The only
way out was to kill the app; the keys in flight then got a strike from the journal, and after two
kills were left out.

What:
- The end of a pass waits at most JOB_WAIT_S in all for the jobs in flight. A wait for a free
  slot (the normal pace of the pass) ends only when no job came back for JOB_WAIT_S. Either way
  the pass stops ("N build(s) in flight did not return for 120 s (...)"), no later pass runs, the
  key being read is handed back, and the game starts.
- The keys in flight get their strike: the pass's journal (.inflight_set) is closed with them
  still in it and is not removed, so the next PrepareShaderSet (the pass beside the game, which
  starts right after, or the next start) adds one strike each, exactly as after a kill. They are
  also left out for the rest of this session (shader_set_abandoned_keys). A key that hangs at two
  starts is struck out for good, as before.
- How the abandoned jobs stay safe (nothing they still touch is freed):
  * The jobs' shared state (mutex, condition, counts, free slots, slot keys, journal stream) is
    on the heap in a std::shared_ptr that every job holds by value; it was on
    PrepareShaderSet's stack before. note_slot is copied into each job and holds the state's
    address; `seconds` captures the start time by value.
  * The ShaderSetPass is made with std::make_shared in RunShaderSetPrepass (it derives from
    enable_shared_from_this) and every job holds a shared pointer to it: its counters stay valid.
    The jobs never touch `left`, the list sets or the reporter, which stay on the stack.
  * A late job closes its slot on the closed journal stream: nothing is written (the strike
    stays), and its counts go to the abandoned state.
  * The prepass's thread pool is not joined (its destructor would wait for the stuck build): a
    guard hands it to the cache (shader_set_abandoned_pools), also when the function throws.
    ~PipelineCache waits for those pools first, before anything else, so a late build never
    meets a destroyed cache, store or device. Cost: a build that never returns at all now holds
    the exit instead of the boot (the runner's kill ends it, as it ended the boot before).
  * The store: Add, AddNotKeepable, Commit and Compact all take the store's mutex, so a late
    Add beside the next pass is safe (Compact only drops the dedupe maps, rebuilt on demand).
  * Not covered: if putting the pool in that list throws (no memory for one pointer), the pool is
    leaked unjoined, and a build that returns during the exit could then touch the cache.
- The start line of the prepass names the limit.

Needs 0770-0772 and 0780-0785 (it changes their job captures). Checked: apply in name order,
PS5-target syntax check. Not checked: a real stuck build (none can be made on purpose here).
